WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.350
Lithification turns loose sediment into rock.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.430
Rounded gravel produces conglomerate; angular gravel produces breccia.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.550
Sand-sized grains make sandstone. These names describe the preserved clasts, rather

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.230
than a unique depositional setting.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.730
Grain composition and roundness refine the description after the main size-based

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:19.270
classification.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:24.390
Arkose contains at least twenty-five percent sand-sized feldspar in this course

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:29.550
source. Preserved feldspar commonly supports rapid burial after derivation from crystalline

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.230
rocks. Quartz sandstone contains mostly quartz, reflecting a quartz-rich source or

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.170
destruction of weaker grains. Graywacke contains a mixture of mineral and

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.390
rock-fragment compositions.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.510
Siltstone contains silt-sized particles, commonly quartz.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.310
Shale contains very fine, aligned clay-rich material and splits into thin

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.710
sheets or chips. That splitting behavior matters when distinguishing shale from

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.870
other mudrocks. Use the source photographs with the texture descriptions, rather

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.450
than choosing a rock from color alone.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.950
Burial adds weight. During compaction, grains move closer together, pore space

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.330
shrinks, and water or air is expelled.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.770
Platy clay particles can rotate toward parallel orientations as a layer

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.910
thins. The grains remain solid:

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.930
compaction is packing, not melting, and some pore space can remain

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.150
even after burial.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.630
Water moving through connected pores carries dissolved components.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.530
New minerals precipitate around grains and bind them as cement.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.150
Four common cements here are calcite, silica, clay minerals and iron

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.550
oxides. Silica can make a resistant rock, calcite can redissolve, and

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.210
iron oxides commonly redden it.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.090
Cementation and compaction are different contributions to lithification.
